Draft for discussion. This proposes adopting Mimblewimble Extension Blocks (MWEB), derived from Litecoin's implementation, as an opt-in confidential transaction facility for Dogecoin. A working port exists and is linked below. The port diverges from Litecoin's ownership cryptography in ways this document records rather than hides, and the scheme has not been reviewed by anyone other than its author. Nothing here should be read as a recommendation to deploy on a schedule.

== Abstract ==

MWEB moves transactions into a separate block attached to each canonical block, where amounts are Pedersen commitments and ownership is proven by signature rather than by script. Value enters through a peg-in and leaves through a peg-out, and the canonical chain sees only the aggregate. Litecoin activated it as a soft fork in 2022.

This DIP proposes the same structure for Dogecoin, with three things stated up front. Dogecoin cannot use BIP9 to activate it, for the same reason it cannot use BIP9 for SegWit: the chain ID occupies the <code>nVersion</code> bits versionbits signals in. MWEB depends on SegWit, so it cannot activate first. And the port's ownership cryptography is not a transliteration of Litecoin's: one key class Litecoin uses is absent on the reference branch, which changes what the balance equations prove. A second branch implements it, so the two models can be compared directly.

== Motivation ==

Dogecoin has no confidentiality. Every amount and every link between inputs and outputs is public forever, which is a privacy property most users do not knowingly choose and cannot opt out of.

MWEB is the only deployed design for adding optional confidentiality to a UTXO chain as a soft fork, with a production implementation on a chain Dogecoin is already merge-mined alongside. The alternative designs either require a hard fork, require trusted setup, or exist only on chains with very different assumptions.

The case against is equally plain and belongs in the same paragraph. It is a large amount of new consensus code, it vendors a cryptographic library, it adds a second place where funds can live, and confidential transactions are harder to audit precisely because they are confidential. This DIP exists so those tradeoffs are argued before anything ships, not after.

== What MWEB is ==

Each canonical block may carry an extension block. The extension block holds inputs, outputs and kernels. Outputs commit to amounts as Pedersen commitments with Bulletproof range proofs. Kernels carry fees, peg-ins, peg-outs and lock heights, and are signed by their own excess.

The canonical chain and the extension block are tied together by the HogEx, a transaction at the end of <code>block.vtx</code> whose output script carries the extension block's header hash. Peg-in outputs in canonical transactions pay into MWEB; the HogEx spends them and pays out the peg-outs, so the canonical chain sees a single transaction whose net effect is the extension block's supply change.

The supply invariant is that the sum of commitments balances against the kernel offset once fees, peg-ins and peg-outs are accounted for. A node that validates this does not learn any individual amount, but does learn that nothing was created.

== What the port implements and what it does not ==

Stated plainly, because "derived from Litecoin's MWEB" could mean anything from a transliteration to an approximation.

Present and working: the extension block with inputs, outputs and kernels; Pedersen commitments with Bulletproof range proofs; kernel, input and output signatures; peg-in and peg-out; the HogEx binding; append-only output and kernel MMRs with a leafset bitmap for the unspent set; stealth addresses for one-sided payments; and offset aggregation, so a block carrying several MWEB transactions balances against one header offset.

Absent relative to Litecoin on the reference branch: the MuSig-style input key aggregation, discussed below. It is not a missing feature so much as a different key model, and the field it would use is still parsed and carried on the wire. The kernel stealth-excess weighting, previously also absent, is now implemented and enforced.

Where the port differs in form rather than substance: Litecoin carries running sum state and extends it with the candidate, so staging stays linear in the body size. The port revalidates the whole staged set on each addition, which is quadratic. Both refuse the same transactions; the port does not assume its offset combination is correct, which is the bug the earlier XOR aggregation had. Worth revisiting if block weight ever makes it matter.

Not implemented by either: cut-through at block assembly. An output created and spent within one block appears as both. Litecoin's block builder does not do this either, so it is a property of Mimblewimble in general that neither implementation exercises here.

== Activation ==

=== Why not BIP9 ===

BIP9 signals in the high bits of <code>nVersion</code>. On Dogecoin those bits hold the AuxPoW chain ID: <code>SetBaseVersion</code> packs it at <code>VERSION_CHAIN_START = 1 << 16</code> (<code>primitives/pureheader.h:23,26</code>). Signalling there either collides with the chain ID or requires relocating it, which is a hard fork.

The miner does not signal in any case. <code>ComputeBlockVersion</code> is commented out and the template always carries <code>VERSIONBITS_LAST_OLD_BLOCK_VERSION</code> (<code>miner.cpp:156</code>), so a BIP9 deployment on this chain cannot lock in on any network.

This is the same obstacle the SegWit activation draft describes, and the two should be resolved together rather than separately.

=== What the port uses instead ===

The pre-BIP9 <code>IsSuperMajority</code> mechanism, which compares <code>GetBaseVersion() = nVersion % 256</code> and so never reads the chain ID.

SegWit activates when <code>nSegwitEnforceVersion</code> base-version blocks reach the supermajority at or after <code>nSegwitStartHeight</code>. MWEB sits one rung above at <code>nMwebEnforceVersion >= nSegwitEnforceVersion</code>, so MWEB cannot activate before or without SegWit. Both answers latch, because extension blocks hold funds and a mid-flight deactivation would strand them.

Mainnet thresholds are <code>nMajorityEnforceBlockUpgrade = 1500</code> of <code>nMajorityWindow = 2000</code>, so 75% of a rolling 2000-block window, about a day and a half at target spacing.

The port latches on that 75% threshold, which is not the number the plan of record names. Discussion #2264 proposed "full activation with 1900 blocks having v5 in a 2000 block window", which is 95% and corresponds to <code>nMajorityRejectBlockOutdated</code>. That figure was reaffirmed in February 2023: "I personally don't have an issue with a 95% activation requirement".

Both tiers were live on this chain once. Dogecoin 1.10 enforced new rules on new-version blocks at <code>nMajorityEnforceBlockUpgrade</code> and rejected outdated blocks at <code>nMajorityRejectBlockOutdated</code>. 1.14 moved deployment to height gating through <code>BIP65Height</code>, and <code>nMajorityRejectBlockOutdated</code> has been read nowhere since. Restoring the 95% behaviour therefore means rewiring the rejection path rather than changing a constant, and that work is not in this port.

Until it is, the guarantee is weaker than the plan assumes. Old-version blocks stay valid after the latch, so a base-version-4 block mined after MWEB activates is accepted and simply cannot carry extension block data. This draft records the gap rather than resolving it, because choosing between justifying 75% and implementing 95% belongs to review.

=== The chain as it actually is ===

504,631 blocks from local block files, September 2025 through September 2026:

    base   auxpow  chain      count    share
    4      1       98        504435   99.96%
    4      0       98           193    0.04%

Every block is base version 4. The 193 without the AuxPoW bit are natively mined, also version 4. Chain ID 98 throughout, and no monthly variance at all across those thirteen months.

The only precedent for moving the base version is v3 to v4:

    2019-02   v4 first appears,  1.89%
    2019-09   v4 19.25%
    2020-03   v4 crosses half,  51.81%
    2020-07   v4 70.01%
    2020-11   v4 100%

About twenty-one months from first appearance to universal, thirteen to half.

=== What that means ===

75% of 2000 blocks is a day and a half once the hashrate emits the new version. The window is not the constraint, shipping is. The ladder requires merge-mining pools to deliberately bump the base version in their own software, there is no latent signalling to ride on, and the one time this chain did it the first fifth of hashrate took seven months.

That is a coordination problem rather than a protocol one, and it should be treated as the long pole in any MWEB schedule rather than as a deployment detail.

=== Release timing ===

Previous developer consensus, restated in January 2024, is to do any soft fork separately from the .0 release that bumps seven major Bitcoin Core versions. Nothing here should ride that release. The gate wants a release of its own, once the version bump has settled.

=== Block weight ===

SegWit as copied carries a 4x weight increase. #3911 frames the choice as three options: ship Bitcoin-style activation and take the increase, remove the witness space extension and close that path permanently, or reserve the space without activating it. Closest to consensus in #2264 has been vbyte == byte.

MWEB adds its own accounting on top, because extension block data is weighed separately from the canonical block. This draft assumes vbyte == byte on the canonical side and proposes no block size increase. If the SegWit DIP picks differently, the MWEB weight accounting has to be restated against whatever it picks, so the two cannot be specified independently.

== Divergence from Litecoin ==

The reference branch is not a transliteration. The differences are in ownership cryptography and they change what is proven, so they are stated here rather than left to a reader of the diff.

Litecoin aggregates a fresh per-spend input key with the output's one-time key, <code>K_agg = K_i + H(K_i||K_o)*K_o</code>, and the hash coefficient defends against an attacker-chosen <code>K_i</code>. The reference branch verifies against the output's one-time key alone, and pins the claimed commitment and key to the spent output in consensus state.

This is a narrower departure than it first appears. Litecoin's stealth key feature bit is optional in all three places it could be required: <code>Input::BuildSignedMsg</code> falls back to <code>K_o</code> alone when the bit is clear, <code>TxBody::Validate</code> batch-verifies whatever each input builds without requiring it, and <code>Input::IsStandard</code> admits <code>features = 0x00</code>. Only Litecoin's wallet always sets it. The port therefore runs a configuration upstream's own consensus already validates, rather than one upstream would reject, and it signs strictly more under that configuration: the commitment and the output pubkey are in the port's signed message and absent from Litecoin's.

The pin is connect-only, so it is not what carries the argument at relay. What carries it on both paths is the value balance: an input contributes the spent output's commitment <code>v*H + b*G</code>, and absorbing <code>b*G</code> into a kernel excess requires <code>b</code>, so a party who does not know the blind cannot make the body balance whatever key it declares.

The aggregation is therefore not needed on two independent grounds: upstream consensus accepts the unset-bit key, and knowledge of the blind is required before the key question arises. The second is what the port would rest on if upstream ever made the bit mandatory, and it is the first thing a reviewer should try to break.

Litecoin weights the kernel verification key when a stealth excess is present and feeds both the input stealth keys and the kernel stealth excesses into its stealth balance. The kernel half is now implemented: a kernel carrying the feature bit must satisfy the weighted aggregate <code>X*H(X||X_s) + X_s</code>, its key is subtracted in the stealth balance, and a bit set over an all-zero key is rejected as non-canonical. The coefficient is what makes this load-bearing, since a plain <code>X + X_s</code> is satisfied by knowledge of <code>k</code> alone once the signer picks <code>X_s = K - X</code>, which would close the stealth balance with a key nobody owns. Removing the weighting from the verifier accepts exactly that signature, which a unit test pins by ablation rather than by argument.

The input stealth key <code>K_i</code> remains the one absent class on the reference branch. The field still parses and would be committed by the input signature if the bit were set, so the wire format is compatible, but nothing verifies against it and the wallet never sets it.

The consequence is that the reference branch's stealth balance is Litecoin's equation restricted to the keys that exist there. Remove <code>K_i</code> from Litecoin's <code>sum(K_s) + sum(K_i) == sum(E') + x'*G + sum(K_o)</code> and it reduces to the port's. That is an argument the divergence is coherent, not a proof the scheme is sound.

One divergence remains open rather than argued closed. Litecoin's range proof commits the serialized output message as extra data; the port's does not. The output signature covers the message, so the malleation Litecoin defends against there is closed by a different route, but the two mechanisms are not interchangeable for anything that verifies proofs without also verifying signatures.

=== The two key models side by side ===

Because the input-key argument above is the load-bearing one, the alternative is implemented rather than only described. Two branches differ in exactly that choice and in nothing else:

* <code>mweb-consensus-hardening</code> verifies an input against <code>K_o</code> alone, which is Litecoin's unset-bit branch with a wider signed message. This is the reference branch and the one the rest of this document describes.
* <code>mweb-input-key-aggregation</code> verifies against <code>K_agg = K_i + H(K_i||K_o)*K_o</code>, sets the stealth key bit on every spend, and carries <code>sum(K_i)</code> in the stealth balance, matching Litecoin's wallet behaviour.

The second branch adds no security property over the first, for the reason given above: upstream validates the unset-bit path and the port signs the wider message either way. It exists so a reviewer can diff two models rather than reason about which branch of upstream's rule the port takes. It is 185 lines across 8 files, and it is unbuilt and untested, which its commit message states. A reviewer who prefers that model should treat it as a sketch to be finished, not as an alternative that is ready.

== Security considerations ==

=== Miner opt-in ===

The standing objection to extension blocks on this chain is that they are miner opt-in, put in #2264 as a conceptual issue that "means we weaken security on whatever is touching extensions". It applies here. A miner that does not implement MWEB still produces valid blocks, so the hashrate validating extension block contents is a subset of the hashrate securing the canonical chain, and funds inside the extension block are secured by that subset rather than by all of it.

Litecoin ships with the same property and has run with it since 2022, which is evidence that it is survivable rather than evidence that it is sound. This draft does not resolve the objection. It records that MWEB inherits it, and that the peg-in is the point at which a holder chooses to accept it.

=== Cryptography ===

The cryptography has not been reviewed by anyone other than its author. Everything below is the author's own assessment and should be read as a list of what to attack, not as assurance.

What is closed. Outputs carry a sender signature over the commitment, both keys, the output message and the range proof hash, and consensus rejects an output with no sender key, so a relaying peer cannot rewrite a receiver key, view tag or masked value without invalidating it. An input with no owner key is rejected rather than having its signature skipped. The stealth balance runs on every body rather than only those carrying a stealth key. Connect rejects a null claimed key instead of matching it against an output created without one.

What is open.

The range proof carries no extra commit data, so a verifier that checks proofs without checking signatures sees an unbound message.

<code>TxBuilder</code> derives a sender key by hashing the coin's blind when the caller supplies none. That is deterministic rather than ephemeral and is a stand-in for a wallet-supplied key, not a design decision.

The input stealth key remains parsed and inert on the reference branch. A future change that gives it meaning must account for every body already accepted with it set to nothing. The kernel stealth excess no longer has this problem: it is enforced, and MWEB has never activated on a live network, so there are no bodies to grandfather.

Confidentiality is not anonymity. MWEB hides amounts and breaks the input-output link inside the extension block; it does not hide that a peg-in or peg-out occurred, nor their amounts.

The vendored library. <code>src/secp256k1-zkp</code> is 34,977 of the port's 44,612 added lines. Its provenance is recorded in the branch, but a reviewer should treat a cryptographic library arriving inside a consensus change as the first thing to check rather than the last.

== Backwards compatibility ==

This is a soft fork. Pre-activation nodes see peg-in outputs as anyone-can-spend and the HogEx as an ordinary transaction, and follow the chain without validating extension blocks.

The port is currently inert on live networks. Mainnet and testnet3 both set <code>nMwebStartHeight = 0</code> and <code>nMwebEnforceVersion = 0</code>, so nothing activates until heights and versions are chosen. Only regtest enables it, at height 100 with version 6. Choosing those parameters is a separate decision this DIP does not make.

Wallets that do not implement MWEB continue to work unchanged and simply cannot send to or receive from the extension block.

== Reference implementation ==

Branch [https://github.com/xanimo/dogecoin/tree/mweb-consensus-hardening mweb-consensus-hardening] at <code>xanimo/dogecoin</code>, 86 commits on top of <code>dogecoin/dogecoin</code> master, 233 files, 44,929 insertions of which 34,977 are the <code>secp256k1-zkp</code> subtree. The hand-written surface is roughly 8,500 lines: <code>src/libmw</code> 4,470, <code>src/mweb</code> 1,910, tests 1,681, and the remainder across <code>validation.cpp</code> and <code>net_processing.cpp</code>.

Branch [https://github.com/xanimo/dogecoin/tree/mweb-input-key-aggregation mweb-input-key-aggregation] carries the alternative input key model described above, 185 lines on top of the reference branch. Unbuilt and untested.

Three documents in the branch carry the detail this DIP summarises:

* <code>doc/mweb-activation.md</code>, the activation mechanism and the block version census
* <code>doc/mweb-crypto-divergence.md</code>, the ownership cryptography and what each signature commits to
* <code>doc/mweb-litecoin-fix-triage.md</code>, every Litecoin commit touching MWEB after v0.21.5.6 triaged against this port

Current test state on the reference branch: 295 unit suites pass in the sanitiser tree, including 43 MWEB cases, and <code>mweb-version6-activation.py</code> and <code>mweb-p2p-relay.py</code> both pass in a wallet-enabled tree. The sanitiser tree is configured <code>--disable-wallet</code>, so the two functional tests need a separate build.

The Litecoin fix triage covers five upstream commits after the pinned tag. One is not applicable for absence, the other four are ported, two of them in a different shape than upstream because the port's structure differs. Those shape differences are recorded per commit.

== Open questions for discussion ==

# Does Dogecoin want optional confidentiality at all? Everything else here is downstream of that, and it is a question for the community rather than for this document.
# The activation ladder shares its blocker with SegWit. Should the two be proposed as one deployment, or does MWEB wait until SegWit has activated by whatever mechanism is chosen?
# Who reviews the cryptography? The divergences above are argued, not proven, and the author is not a suitable reviewer of his own scheme.
# Should the range proof commit the output message as extra data, matching Litecoin, even though the output signature already covers it?
# Which input key model should ship? The reference branch takes Litecoin's unset-bit branch and signs more under it; <code>mweb-input-key-aggregation</code> takes Litecoin's wallet behaviour. The first is tested and the second is not.
# Should the inert input stealth key field be removed from the wire format rather than carried as parsed-and-ignored?
# What activation heights and versions, and on what evidence? The census says the window is not the constraint and pool coordination is.
# Should <code>secp256k1-zkp</code> be a subtree, a submodule, or a dependency built through <code>depends</code>?

